WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.560 --> 00:00:03.104
Is that Captain Adam?

00:00:03.104 --> 00:00:05.856
Captain Adam, yes. It's
Captain Adam, holy [bleep].

00:00:05.856 --> 00:00:07.942
The one and only. How’s it going?

00:00:08.359 --> 00:00:11.487
The entire island has only 400 residents,

00:00:11.695 --> 00:00:15.616
so I guess I shouldn't be surprised
when the guy I hitched a ride

00:00:15.616 --> 00:00:18.953
with to get to the island
also runs a local oyster farm.

00:00:19.662 --> 00:00:21.455
A man of many things, apparently.

00:00:21.455 --> 00:00:22.540
Yeah welcome.

00:00:22.540 --> 00:00:24.458
You guys want to check out some oysters?

00:00:24.458 --> 00:00:26.335
Yes, please.

00:00:28.003 --> 00:00:29.213
We need 100.

00:00:29.213 --> 00:00:31.132
For the dinner coming up. Yeah,
we got ‘em right here.

00:00:31.132 --> 00:00:32.758
You want to help me pick them
up? Let's do it.

00:00:32.758 --> 00:00:33.759
All right, you guys start.

00:00:33.759 --> 00:00:35.761
What size are you looking for?

00:00:35.761 --> 00:00:38.931
So we're looking for a two and three
quarter, to three inch oyster. Okay.

00:00:38.931 --> 00:00:42.143
So you got a oyster that's touches on
the bottom and touches here.

00:00:42.434 --> 00:00:44.061
That's a two and three quarter oyster.

00:00:44.061 --> 00:00:46.814
And you do that because you want them
to grow to their full maturity.

00:00:46.856 --> 00:00:47.273
Yeah.

00:00:47.273 --> 00:00:51.193
So when we harvest for you guys,
we just make sure they're all perfect.

00:00:51.318 --> 00:00:52.027
No pressure.

00:00:52.027 --> 00:00:53.195
No pressure, no pressure.

00:00:53.195 --> 00:00:54.780
It's just your debut dinner.

00:00:54.780 --> 00:00:56.407
No big deal, right?

00:00:59.785 --> 00:01:02.329
The oysters are sold all over the country.

00:01:02.454 --> 00:01:07.209
Some of the best restaurants in the world,
you know, French Laundry and all through

00:01:07.209 --> 00:01:10.254
New York and Boston and California,

00:01:10.254 --> 00:01:13.382
because North Haven has beautiful soils,

00:01:13.716 --> 00:01:17.386
the ocean's clean,
and that comes out in the taste.

00:01:17.720 --> 00:01:20.306
And for oyster aficionados,

00:01:21.599 --> 00:01:23.601
it's a damn fine oyster.

00:01:27.104 --> 00:01:30.232
Ow, oh, oh.

00:01:30.816 --> 00:01:32.693
I got bit by a crab
today on the lobster boat.

00:01:32.693 --> 00:01:35.070
Oh, you did? I did. With Abel?

00:01:35.070 --> 00:01:36.322
Were you with Abel? Yeah.

00:01:36.322 --> 00:01:38.532
He took it off, I was like,
get it off my hand.

00:01:41.118 --> 00:01:43.454
We went lobstering and oystering
together for five years.

00:01:43.704 --> 00:01:45.080
It really is a family out here.

00:01:45.080 --> 00:01:47.374
All of you on the island. Yeah.

00:01:48.375 --> 00:01:51.879
In a community
this small, everyone relies on each other.

00:01:52.338 --> 00:01:56.675
And I can see that Turner Farm plays a
huge part in supporting the whole island.

00:01:57.009 --> 00:02:01.096
So everyone here is personally
invested in its success.

00:02:04.225 --> 00:02:06.310
How many you think we
have right now?

00:02:06.310 --> 00:02:07.561
That is definitely over 100.

00:02:07.645 --> 00:02:11.273
Great...10...103 good karma.

00:02:11.273 --> 00:02:13.651
That's cool, yeah. Done.

00:02:13.651 --> 00:02:15.444
All right, you want to taste this?

00:02:15.444 --> 00:02:16.654
I sure do.

00:02:19.949 --> 00:02:21.575
Mm hmm. Right.

00:02:21.575 --> 00:02:25.537
That is one of the most beautifully
salty oysters I've ever had.

00:02:25.746 --> 00:02:26.956
Thank you so much.

00:02:27.122 --> 00:02:28.207
Ever.

00:02:28.207 --> 00:02:30.918
There's been so much hype around
these North Haven oysters.

00:02:30.918 --> 00:02:35.214
I didn't know what to expect,
but I love the sweet, salty creaminess,

00:02:35.214 --> 00:02:39.635
and it's amazing and honestly,
better than I could have ever imagined.

00:02:39.927 --> 00:02:42.263
But I've also spotted something
at Adam's oyster farm

00:02:42.263 --> 00:02:43.931
that I want to share with him.

00:02:44.765 --> 00:02:46.100
Taste this.

00:02:46.100 --> 00:02:47.685
They are really tender, that you
just eat ‘em

00:02:47.685 --> 00:02:48.769
What a little heart?

00:02:48.769 --> 00:02:50.604
What? I’ll try that. I'll try that.

00:02:50.604 --> 00:02:51.772
Thank you.

00:02:52.898 --> 00:02:53.857
Oh, my God. That's good.

00:02:53.857 --> 00:02:55.901
That's fantastic.

00:02:55.901 --> 00:02:57.444
They're like little, tiny gherkins.

00:02:58.529 --> 00:02:59.446
Oh, and I like that crunch

00:02:59.446 --> 00:03:01.824
sound so satisfying.

00:03:02.283 --> 00:03:04.410
Think about
if you dehydrated this whole thing

00:03:04.410 --> 00:03:07.621
and deep fried the whole thing
and how the whole thing would just pop up.

00:03:07.621 --> 00:03:08.998
You had me at deep fried.

00:03:08.998 --> 00:03:11.625
Carolynn has a plan for her oysters
in this great hot sauce,

00:03:11.625 --> 00:03:14.878
but I'm kind of inspired
by this seaweed project.

00:03:15.045 --> 00:03:16.130
I'm going to call it a project for now

00:03:16.130 --> 00:03:18.841
because I still have to figure out
what what I'm going to do exactly.

00:03:19.508 --> 00:03:22.678
I want to do something unique, surprising,

00:03:22.761 --> 00:03:26.849
something that will really add to what
Carolynn is already doing at Turner Farm.

00:03:26.849 --> 00:03:30.102
No clue what it's going to be
yet, but I'll figure it out.

